==English==
[[File:Sawhorse.svg|thumb|saw-trestle or [[sawhorse]] (1,2)]]
[[File:Wooden trestle bridge approach.JPG|thumb|wooden [[trestle bridge]] (3,4)]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|trestel}}, from {{der|en|fro|trestel|t=crossbeam}} ({{cog|fr|tréteau}}), from {{der|en|la|trānstellum}}, diminutive of {{m|la|trānstrum}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɹɛsəl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-trestle.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛsəl|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[horizontal]] member [[supported]] near each end by a [[pair]] of [[divergent]] [[leg]]s, such as [[sawhorse]]s.
# A [[folding]] or [[fixed]] set of legs used to support a [[tabletop]] or [[planks]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1900|author={{w|Charles W. Chesnutt}}|title=The House Behind the Cedars|chapter=I|passage=He turned the knob, but the door was locked. Retracing his steps past a vacant lot, the young man entered a shop where a colored man was employed in varnishing a coffin, which stood on two '''trestles''' in the middle of the floor.}}
# A [[framework]], using [[spreading]], divergent pairs of legs used to support a [[bridge]].
# A [[trestle bridge]].

